### [🖼️ Rendered](https://github.com/Centril/rfcs/blob/rfc/mut-pattern-shorthand/text/0000-mut-pattern-shorthand.md)

### 📝 Summary

This RFC aims to improve ergonomics by allowing you to write:

```rust
// tuples:
let mut (x, y, z) = foo;

// arrays:
let mut [a, b, c] = arr;
```

### 💖 Thanks

To @scottmcm, @varkor and others for their review and interesting discussions.

Proposes a lint on side effect free const fn invocations that are not used by the program.

[Rendered](https://github.com/est31/rfcs/blob/unused_const_fn_result_lint/text/0000-unused_const_fn_result_lint.md)

An example would be:
```rust
const fn add_one(v: u32) -> u32 {
    v + 1
}
fn foo() {
    add_one(2); // WARNING unused result of const fn
}
```

Thanks go to @Centril and @rkruppe who reviewed the drafts.

This RFC propose to add two new methods to the slice, the `group_by` and `group_by_mut`. These two will provide a way to iterate over non-overlapping sub-slices of a base slice that are separated by the predicate given by the user (e.g. `Partial::eq`, `|a, b| a < b`).

The predicate is called on two elements following themselves, it means the predicate is called on `slice[0]` and `slice[1]` then on `slice[1]` and `slice[2]`...

```rust
let slice = &[1, 1, 1, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2];

let mut iter = slice.group_by(|a, b| a == b);

assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[1, 1, 1][..]));
assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[3, 3][..]));
assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[2, 2, 2][..]));
assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);
```

[Pending Pull Request](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/51606)

[Work around temporary library](https://github.com/Kerollmops/slice-group-by)

[Rendered](https://github.com/Kerollmops/rust-rfcs/blob/group-by/text/0000-group-by.md)

Make disambiguating generic arguments in expressions with `::` optional, allowing generic arguments to be specified without `::` (making the "turbofish" notation no longer necessary).

[Rendered](https://github.com/varkor/rfcs/blob/undisambiguated_expr_generics/text/0000-undisambiguated-expr-generics.md)

This makes the following valid syntax:

```rust
struct Nooper<T>(T);

impl<T> Nooper<T> {
    fn noop<U>(&self, _: U) {}
}

fn id<T>(t: T) -> T {
    t
}

fn main() {
    id<u32>(0u32); // ok
    let n = Nooper<&str>(":)"); // ok
    n.noop<()>(()); // ok
}
```

Thanks to @Centril, @scottmcm, @joshtriplett and @rpjohnst for feedback!
